<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-further-applications" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Further Applications: Heat, Levers, Triangles, and Magic Squares</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Polynomial interpolation was our first example of a problem that becomes a system
      of linear equations once the right unknowns have been named. This section collects
      four more, from physics, geometry, and recreational mathematics. In each of them the
      real work is the modeling: deciding what the unknowns are and translating the
      problem into equations. After that, the row reduction algorithm of
      <xref ref="sec-row-reduction" text="type-global"/> takes over.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each example also carries a lesson about reading the answer. Row reduction
      describes every solution of the linear system, and it knows nothing else about the
      problem. Whether a solution makes sense <mdash/> a weight must be positive, the
      entries of a magic square must be different whole numbers <mdash/> is a separate
      question, and it is ours to ask.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-heat-plate">
    <title>Temperatures on a Plate</title>

    <p>
      A thin square metal plate is insulated on both faces, and each of its four edges is
      held at a constant temperature. After a while the temperature at every point of the
      plate stops changing, and we would like to know these equilibrium temperatures. A
      simple model replaces the plate by a grid of points and uses the following rule.
    </p>

    <principle xml:id="prin-averaging-rule">
      <title>The Averaging Rule</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          At equilibrium, the temperature at each interior grid point is the average of
          the temperatures at its four nearest grid points: the ones directly above it,
          below it, to its left, and to its right.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </principle>

    <figure xml:id="fig-heat-plate">
      <caption>
        A square plate whose edges are held at <m>80^\circ</m>, <m>60^\circ</m>,
        <m>40^\circ</m>, and <m>20^\circ</m>, with a grid of four interior points. Each
        interior point has two neighbors on the edges and two neighbors among the other
        interior points.
      </caption>
      <image width="52%">
        <shortdescription>A square plate with a two-by-two grid of interior points labeled x1, x2 in the top row and x3, x4 in the bottom row; the top edge is at 80 degrees, the right edge at 60, the bottom edge at 40, and the left edge at 20.</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-heat-plate" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
          <diagram dimensions="(340,340)" margins="8">
            <coordinates bbox="(-0.95,-0.95,3.95,3.95)">
              <group at="heat-plate">
                <rectangle at="plate-outline" lower-left="(0,0)" dimensions="(3,3)" fill="#EDF0E6" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                <group at="grid-lines">
                  <line endpoints="((1,0),(1,3))" stroke="#808080" thickness="1" dash="4 3"/>
                  <line endpoints="((2,0),(2,3))" stroke="#808080" thickness="1" dash="4 3"/>
                  <line endpoints="((0,1),(3,1))" stroke="#808080" thickness="1" dash="4 3"/>
                  <line endpoints="((0,2),(3,2))" stroke="#808080" thickness="1" dash="4 3"/>
                </group>
                <group at="edge-top">
                  <point p="(1,3)" style="box" size="4" fill="#606060"/>
                  <point p="(2,3)" style="box" size="4" fill="#606060"/>
                  <label anchor="(1.5,3)" alignment="north" offset="(0,5)"><m>80^\circ</m></label>
                </group>
                <group at="edge-right">
                  <point p="(3,2)" style="box" size="4" fill="#606060"/>
                  <point p="(3,1)" style="box" size="4" fill="#606060"/>
                  <label anchor="(3,1.5)" alignment="east" offset="(7,0)"><m>60^\circ</m></label>
                </group>
                <group at="edge-bottom">
                  <point p="(1,0)" style="box" size="4" fill="#606060"/>
                  <point p="(2,0)" style="box" size="4" fill="#606060"/>
                  <label anchor="(1.5,0)" alignment="south" offset="(0,-5)"><m>40^\circ</m></label>
                </group>
                <group at="edge-left">
                  <point p="(0,2)" style="box" size="4" fill="#606060"/>
                  <point p="(0,1)" style="box" size="4" fill="#606060"/>
                  <label anchor="(0,1.5)" alignment="west" offset="(-7,0)"><m>20^\circ</m></label>
                </group>
                <point at="heat-x1" p="(1,2)" size="5" fill="#5E011C" alignment="northeast"><m>x_1</m></point>
                <point at="heat-x2" p="(2,2)" size="5" fill="#5E011C" alignment="northeast"><m>x_2</m></point>
                <point at="heat-x3" p="(1,1)" size="5" fill="#5E011C" alignment="northeast"><m>x_3</m></point>
                <point at="heat-x4" p="(2,1)" size="5" fill="#5E011C" alignment="northeast"><m>x_4</m></point>
              </group>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="heat-plate" text="A square plate with a grid: four interior points with unknown temperatures and eight edge points with known temperatures">
                <annotation ref="plate-outline" text="The outline of the square plate"/>
                <annotation ref="grid-lines" text="Two vertical and two horizontal dashed grid lines, which cross at the four interior points and meet the edges at the eight edge points"/>
                <annotation ref="edge-top" text="The top edge, held at 80 degrees, with two grid points"/>
                <annotation ref="edge-right" text="The right edge, held at 60 degrees, with two grid points"/>
                <annotation ref="edge-bottom" text="The bottom edge, held at 40 degrees, with two grid points"/>
                <annotation ref="edge-left" text="The left edge, held at 20 degrees, with two grid points"/>
                <annotation ref="heat-x1" text="Upper left interior point, temperature x 1. Its neighbors are the top edge at 80 degrees, the left edge at 20 degrees, x 2 to its right, and x 3 below it."/>
                <annotation ref="heat-x2" text="Upper right interior point, temperature x 2. Its neighbors are the top edge at 80 degrees, the right edge at 60 degrees, x 1 to its left, and x 4 below it."/>
                <annotation ref="heat-x3" text="Lower left interior point, temperature x 3. Its neighbors are the left edge at 20 degrees, the bottom edge at 40 degrees, x 1 above it, and x 4 to its right."/>
                <annotation ref="heat-x4" text="Lower right interior point, temperature x 4. Its neighbors are the right edge at 60 degrees, the bottom edge at 40 degrees, x 2 above it, and x 3 to its left."/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <example xml:id="eg-heat-plate">
      <title>Four Temperatures on a Square Plate</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The plate in <xref ref="fig-heat-plate" text="type-global"/> carries a grid with
          four interior points, whose equilibrium temperatures <m>x_1, x_2, x_3, x_4</m> are
          unknown. The top edge is held at <m>80^\circ</m>, the right edge at
          <m>60^\circ</m>, the bottom edge at <m>40^\circ</m>, and the left edge at
          <m>20^\circ</m>. Use the averaging rule to find the four temperatures.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The upper left point has the top edge above it, the left edge to its left, and the
          interior points with temperatures <m>x_2</m> and <m>x_3</m> to its right and below
          it. The other three points are similar, and the averaging rule gives one equation
          for each interior point:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              x_1 \amp = \tfrac{1}{4}(80 + 20 + x_2 + x_3), \amp x_2 \amp = \tfrac{1}{4}(80 + 60 + x_1 + x_4),
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x_3 \amp = \tfrac{1}{4}(20 + 40 + x_1 + x_4), \amp x_4 \amp = \tfrac{1}{4}(60 + 40 + x_2 + x_3).
            </mrow>
          </md>
          Multiplying each equation by <m>4</m> and collecting the unknowns on the left puts
          the system in standard form:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              4x_1 - x_2 - x_3 \phantom{{}- x_4} \amp = 100
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              -x_1 + 4x_2 \phantom{{}- x_3} - x_4 \amp = 140
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              -x_1 \phantom{{}+ 4x_2} + 4x_3 - x_4 \amp = 60
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \phantom{-x_1} - x_2 - x_3 + 4x_4 \amp = 100
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Now row reduce. A little planning keeps fractions out of the forward phase. (When
          two operations are attached to one arrow, the upper one is performed first.)
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \left[ \begin{array}{cccc|c}
                4 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp 100 \\
                -1 \amp 4 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 140 \\
                -1 \amp 0 \amp 4 \amp -1 \amp 60 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp 4 \amp 100
              \end{array} \right]
              \amp \xrightarrow[-r_1]{r_1 \leftrightarrow r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{cccc|c}
                1 \amp -4 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -140 \\
                4 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp 100 \\
                -1 \amp 0 \amp 4 \amp -1 \amp 60 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp 4 \amp 100
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow[r_1 + r_3]{-4r_1 + r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{cccc|c}
                1 \amp -4 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -140 \\
                0 \amp 15 \amp -1 \amp -4 \amp 660 \\
                0 \amp -4 \amp 4 \amp 0 \amp -80 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp 4 \amp 100
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow[r_2 \leftrightarrow r_3]{-\frac{1}{4} r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{cccc|c}
                1 \amp -4 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -140 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp 20 \\
                0 \amp 15 \amp -1 \amp -4 \amp 660 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp 4 \amp 100
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow[r_2 + r_4]{-15r_2 + r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{cccc|c}
                1 \amp -4 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -140 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp 20 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 14 \amp -4 \amp 360 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp -2 \amp 4 \amp 120
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow[r_3 \leftrightarrow r_4]{-\frac{1}{2} r_4}
              \left[ \begin{array}{cccc|c}
                1 \amp -4 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -140 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp 20 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -2 \amp -60 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 14 \amp -4 \amp 360
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-14r_3 + r_4}
              \left[ \begin{array}{cccc|c}
                1 \amp -4 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -140 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp 20 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -2 \amp -60 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 24 \amp 1200
              \end{array} \right].
            </mrow>
          </md>
          There are four pivots and no free variables, so the solution is unique. Back
          substitution gives
          <md>
            <mrow>
              x_4 \amp = \tfrac{1200}{24} = 50, \amp x_3 \amp = -60 + 2x_4 = 40,
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x_2 \amp = 20 + x_3 = 60, \amp x_1 \amp = -140 + 4x_2 - x_4 = 50.
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          The equilibrium temperatures are <m>50^\circ</m>, <m>60^\circ</m>,
          <m>40^\circ</m>, and <m>50^\circ</m>. As a check, the neighbors of the upper right
          point average to <m>\tfrac{1}{4}(80 + 60 + 50 + 50) = 60</m>. The answer is also
          plausible. All four values lie between the coldest edge and the warmest, as they
          must, since an average never falls outside the range of the numbers averaged. The
          warmest interior point, <m>x_2</m>, is the one next to the two warmest edges, and
          the coolest, <m>x_3</m>, is next to the two coolest.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
      Four grid points give only a rough picture of the plate. A finer grid gives a better
      one, at a price: with <m>100</m> interior points in each direction there are
      <m>10{,}000</m> unknowns and <m>10{,}000</m> equations. Nobody solves such a system by
      hand, but the algorithm is the same, and this is the kind of problem for which it
      was designed. Notice also that every equation, however fine the grid, involves at
      most five of the unknowns.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-levers">
    <title>Balancing Levers</title>

    <p>
      A lever is a rigid bar that is free to turn about one of its points, the pivot.
      Archimedes found the rule that decides when it stays level.
    </p>

    <principle xml:id="prin-law-of-the-lever">
      <title>The Law of the Lever</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Two weights hanging from a lever, on opposite sides of the pivot, balance exactly
          when the products
          <md>
            (\text{weight}) \times (\text{distance from the pivot})
          </md>
          are equal on the two sides.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </principle>

    <figure xml:id="fig-lever-mobile">
      <caption>
        A mobile made of three levers. The numbers above each bar are the distances from
        that bar's pivot to the points where its loads hang.
      </caption>
      <image width="88%">
        <shortdescription>A hanging mobile: a main bar suspended from the ceiling with arms of length 4 and 3; from its left end hangs a bar with arms 1 and 2 carrying weights w1 and w2, and from its right end hangs a bar with arms 1 and 3 carrying weights w3 and w4.</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-lever-mobile" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
          <diagram dimensions="(460,231)" margins="8">
            <coordinates bbox="(-5.9,1.7,6.9,7.9)">
              <group at="mobile">
                <group at="main-lever">
                  <line endpoints="((-1.2,7.6),(1.2,7.6))" stroke="#606060" thickness="5"/>
                  <line endpoints="((0,7.6),(0,6.4))" stroke="#606060" thickness="1.5"/>
                  <line endpoints="((-4,6.4),(3,6.4))" stroke="black" thickness="4"/>
                  <point p="(0,6.4)" size="4" fill="#5E011C"/>
                  <label anchor="(-2,6.4)" alignment="north" offset="(0,3)"><m>4</m></label>
                  <label anchor="(1.5,6.4)" alignment="north" offset="(0,3)"><m>3</m></label>
                </group>
                <group at="left-lever">
                  <line endpoints="((-4,6.4),(-4,4.6))" stroke="#606060" thickness="1.5"/>
                  <line endpoints="((-5,4.6),(-2,4.6))" stroke="black" thickness="4"/>
                  <point p="(-4,4.6)" size="4" fill="#5E011C"/>
                  <label anchor="(-4.5,4.6)" alignment="north" offset="(0,3)"><m>1</m></label>
                  <label anchor="(-3,4.6)" alignment="north" offset="(0,3)"><m>2</m></label>
                </group>
                <group at="right-lever">
                  <line endpoints="((3,6.4),(3,4.6))" stroke="#606060" thickness="1.5"/>
                  <line endpoints="((2,4.6),(6,4.6))" stroke="black" thickness="4"/>
                  <point p="(3,4.6)" size="4" fill="#5E011C"/>
                  <label anchor="(2.5,4.6)" alignment="north" offset="(0,3)"><m>1</m></label>
                  <label anchor="(4.5,4.6)" alignment="north" offset="(0,3)"><m>3</m></label>
                </group>
                <group at="weight-1">
                  <line endpoints="((-5,4.6),(-5,3.4))" stroke="#606060" thickness="1.5"/>
                  <rectangle center="(-5,2.85)" dimensions="(1,1.1)" fill="#DDE2CF" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2"/>
                  <label anchor="(-5,2.85)" alignment="center"><m>w_1</m></label>
                </group>
                <group at="weight-2">
                  <line endpoints="((-2,4.6),(-2,3.4))" stroke="#606060" thickness="1.5"/>
                  <rectangle center="(-2,2.85)" dimensions="(1,1.1)" fill="#DDE2CF" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2"/>
                  <label anchor="(-2,2.85)" alignment="center"><m>w_2</m></label>
                </group>
                <group at="weight-3">
                  <line endpoints="((2,4.6),(2,3.4))" stroke="#606060" thickness="1.5"/>
                  <rectangle center="(2,2.85)" dimensions="(1,1.1)" fill="#DDE2CF" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2"/>
                  <label anchor="(2,2.85)" alignment="center"><m>w_3</m></label>
                </group>
                <group at="weight-4">
                  <line endpoints="((6,4.6),(6,3.4))" stroke="#606060" thickness="1.5"/>
                  <rectangle center="(6,2.85)" dimensions="(1,1.1)" fill="#DDE2CF" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2"/>
                  <label anchor="(6,2.85)" alignment="center"><m>w_4</m></label>
                </group>
              </group>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="mobile" text="A hanging mobile made of three levers and four weights">
                <annotation ref="main-lever" text="The main bar, hanging from the ceiling by a string at its pivot. Its left arm has length 4 and its right arm has length 3. A smaller bar hangs from each end."/>
                <annotation ref="left-lever" text="The left bar, hanging from the left end of the main bar. Weight w 1 hangs at distance 1 to the left of its pivot, and weight w 2 at distance 2 to the right."/>
                <annotation ref="right-lever" text="The right bar, hanging from the right end of the main bar. Weight w 3 hangs at distance 1 to the left of its pivot, and weight w 4 at distance 3 to the right."/>
                <annotation ref="weight-1" text="Weight w 1, at the left end of the left bar"/>
                <annotation ref="weight-2" text="Weight w 2, at the right end of the left bar"/>
                <annotation ref="weight-3" text="Weight w 3, at the left end of the right bar"/>
                <annotation ref="weight-4" text="Weight w 4, at the right end of the right bar"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <example xml:id="eg-lever-mobile">
      <title>A Mobile That Balances</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The mobile in <xref ref="fig-lever-mobile" text="type-global"/> consists of three
          levers. The main bar hangs from the ceiling, and two smaller bars hang from its
          ends, at distances <m>4</m> and <m>3</m> from its pivot. The left bar carries
          weights <m>w_1</m> and <m>w_2</m> at distances <m>1</m> and <m>2</m> from its
          pivot, and the right bar carries weights <m>w_3</m> and <m>w_4</m> at distances
          <m>1</m> and <m>3</m> from its pivot. The bars and strings are light enough to
          ignore.
          <ol marker="(a)">
            <li><p>Find all weights <m>w_1, w_2, w_3, w_4</m> for which all three levers balance.</p></li>
            <li><p>Find the weights if the whole mobile is to weigh <m>14</m> ounces.</p></li>
            <li><p>Can the mobile balance with four equal weights?</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          (a) The law of the lever gives one equation for each bar. For the left bar,
          <m>1 \cdot w_1 = 2 \cdot w_2</m>, and for the right bar,
          <m>1 \cdot w_3 = 3 \cdot w_4</m>. Each end of the main bar carries the total
          weight hanging below it, so the main bar balances when
          <m>4(w_1 + w_2) = 3(w_3 + w_4)</m>. In standard form,
          <md>
            <mrow>
              w_1 - 2w_2 \phantom{{}- 3w_3 - 3w_4} \amp = 0
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              w_3 - 3w_4 \amp = 0
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              4w_1 + 4w_2 - 3w_3 - 3w_4 \amp = 0
            </mrow>
          </md>
          and row reduction gives
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \left[ \begin{array}{cccc|c}
                1 \amp -2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -3 \amp 0 \\
                4 \amp 4 \amp -3 \amp -3 \amp 0
              \end{array} \right]
              \amp \xrightarrow[r_2 \leftrightarrow r_3]{-4r_1 + r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{cccc|c}
                1 \amp -2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 12 \amp -3 \amp -3 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -3 \amp 0
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow[\frac{1}{12} r_2]{3r_3 + r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{cccc|c}
                1 \amp -2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -3 \amp 0
              \end{array} \right]
              \xrightarrow{2r_2 + r_1}
              \left[ \begin{array}{cccc|c}
                1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -2 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -3 \amp 0
              \end{array} \right].
            </mrow>
          </md>
          The variable <m>w_4</m> is free. Setting <m>w_4 = t</m>,
          <md>
            w_1 = 2t, \qquad w_2 = t, \qquad w_3 = 3t, \qquad w_4 = t.
          </md>
          The linear system has a solution for every real <m>t</m>, but a weight must be
          positive, so the mobile balances exactly when <m>t > 0</m>. We could have
          predicted infinitely many solutions: there are more unknowns than equations, and
          the system is consistent because the empty mobile, with every weight zero, is a
          solution. It also makes physical sense. Doubling every weight doubles both sides
          of each balance equation, so only the proportions <m>2 : 1 : 3 : 1</m> matter.
        </p>
        <p>
          (b) The total weight is <m>2t + t + 3t + t = 7t</m>, so <m>7t = 14</m> and
          <m>t = 2</m>: the weights are <m>4</m>, <m>2</m>, <m>6</m>, and <m>2</m> ounces.
          In terms of the system, we have added a fourth equation,
          <m>w_1 + w_2 + w_3 + w_4 = 14</m>, and the solution is now unique.
        </p>
        <p>
          (c) No. Equal weights would require <m>w_1 = w_2</m>, that is, <m>2t = t</m>, which
          forces <m>t = 0</m>. With these distances, the only way to have four equal weights
          is to have no weights at all.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-law-of-cosines">
    <title>The Law of Cosines</title>

    <p>
      In a triangle with sides <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, <m>c</m>, let <m>\alpha</m>,
      <m>\beta</m>, <m>\gamma</m> be the angles opposite those sides, as in
      <xref ref="fig-law-of-cosines" text="type-global"/>. The perpendicular from the
      vertex <m>A</m> meets the opposite side at a point <m>D</m> and cuts that side into
      two pieces. The two right triangles in the figure show that the pieces have lengths
      <m>c\cos\beta</m> and <m>b\cos\gamma</m>, so
      <m>a = b\cos\gamma + c\cos\beta</m>. Dropping perpendiculars from the other two
      vertices gives two more equations of the same kind:
      <md>
        <mrow>
          c\cos\beta + b\cos\gamma \amp = a
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          c\cos\alpha + a\cos\gamma \amp = b
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          b\cos\alpha + a\cos\beta \amp = c
        </mrow>
      </md>
      (If one of the angles is larger than <m>90^\circ</m>, a perpendicular lands outside
      the triangle and one cosine is negative, but the three equations remain true.) Regard
      the sides as known. Then these are three <em>linear</em> equations in the three
      unknowns <m>\cos\alpha</m>, <m>\cos\beta</m>, <m>\cos\gamma</m>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-law-of-cosines">
      <caption>
        The perpendicular from <m>A</m> cuts the side <m>a</m> into pieces of lengths
        <m>c\cos\beta</m> and <m>b\cos\gamma</m>. The triangle is drawn with
        <m>a = 14</m>, <m>b = 15</m>, <m>c = 13</m>; its height is <m>12</m>.
      </caption>
      <image width="58%">
        <shortdescription>Triangle ABC with base BC of length a, left side c, right side b, and angles alpha at A, beta at B, gamma at C. A dashed perpendicular from A meets the base at D, dividing it into a left piece c cosine beta and a right piece b cosine gamma.</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-law-of-cosines" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
          <diagram dimensions="(380,360)" margins="8">
            <coordinates bbox="(-2,-3.7,16,13.3)">
              <group at="cosine-triangle">
                <polygon at="triangle-ABC" points="((0,0),(14,0),(5,12))" closed="yes" fill="#EDF0E6" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2.5"/>
                <line at="altitude-AD" endpoints="((5,12),(5,0))" stroke="#606060" thickness="1.5" dash="6 4"/>
                <angle-marker at="right-angle-D" points="((14,0),(5,0),(5,12))" radius="12" stroke="#606060" thickness="1.5"/>
                <group at="angle-alpha">
                  <angle-marker points="((0,0),(5,12),(14,0))" radius="34" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="1.5"/>
                  <label anchor="(5.95,9.55)" alignment="center"><m>\alpha</m></label>
                </group>
                <angle-marker at="angle-beta" points="((14,0),(0,0),(5,12))" radius="28" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="1.5"><m>\beta</m></angle-marker>
                <angle-marker at="angle-gamma" points="((5,12),(14,0),(0,0))" radius="34" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="1.5"><m>\gamma</m></angle-marker>
                <group at="vertices">
                  <label anchor="(5,12)" alignment="north" offset="(0,4)"><m>A</m></label>
                  <label anchor="(0,0)" alignment="west" offset="(-5,0)"><m>B</m></label>
                  <label anchor="(14,0)" alignment="east" offset="(5,0)"><m>C</m></label>
                  <label anchor="(5,0)" alignment="south" offset="(0,-4)"><m>D</m></label>
                </group>
                <label at="side-c" anchor="(2.5,6)" alignment="northwest" offset="(-3,3)"><m>c</m></label>
                <label at="side-b" anchor="(9.5,6)" alignment="northeast" offset="(3,3)"><m>b</m></label>
                <label at="piece-BD" anchor="(2.4,0)" alignment="south" offset="(0,-5)"><m>c\cos\beta</m></label>
                <label at="piece-DC" anchor="(9.5,0)" alignment="south" offset="(0,-5)"><m>b\cos\gamma</m></label>
                <group at="side-a">
                  <line endpoints="((0,-2.6),(14,-2.6))" stroke="#606060" thickness="1" arrows="2"/>
                  <label anchor="(7,-2.6)" alignment="south" offset="(0,-3)"><m>a</m></label>
                </group>
              </group>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="cosine-triangle" text="A triangle A B C with the perpendicular from A to the opposite side">
                <annotation ref="triangle-ABC" text="Triangle with vertex B at the lower left, vertex C at the lower right, and vertex A at the top, to the left of center"/>
                <annotation ref="vertices" text="The labels of the vertices A, B, C and of the point D on the base, directly below A"/>
                <annotation ref="side-c" text="The left side, from A to B, has length c"/>
                <annotation ref="side-b" text="The right side, from A to C, has length b"/>
                <annotation ref="side-a" text="The base, from B to C, has length a"/>
                <annotation ref="angle-alpha" text="The angle alpha at the top vertex A, opposite the base a"/>
                <annotation ref="angle-beta" text="The angle beta at the vertex B, opposite the side b"/>
                <annotation ref="angle-gamma" text="The angle gamma at the vertex C, opposite the side c"/>
                <annotation ref="altitude-AD" text="A dashed perpendicular from A down to the point D on the base"/>
                <annotation ref="right-angle-D" text="The perpendicular meets the base at a right angle at D"/>
                <annotation ref="piece-BD" text="The piece of the base from B to D has length c times cosine beta"/>
                <annotation ref="piece-DC" text="The piece of the base from D to C has length b times cosine gamma"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <example xml:id="eg-law-of-cosines">
      <title>Solving for the Cosines</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the three equations above for <m>\cos\alpha</m>, <m>\cos\beta</m>, and
          <m>\cos\gamma</m> in terms of the sides <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, <m>c</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          With the unknowns in the order <m>\cos\alpha</m>, <m>\cos\beta</m>,
          <m>\cos\gamma</m>, the augmented matrix is
          <m>\left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c} 0 \amp c \amp b \amp a \\ c \amp 0 \amp a \amp b \\ b \amp a \amp 0 \amp c \end{array} \right]</m>.
          Its entries are letters, and row reducing it means dividing by them. (That is
          legitimate here, since side lengths are positive.) A change of unknowns makes the
          arithmetic much friendlier. Multiply the three equations by <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>,
          and <m>c</m>, respectively, and put
          <md>
            u = bc\cos\alpha, \qquad v = ca\cos\beta, \qquad w = ab\cos\gamma.
          </md>
          The system becomes
          <md>
            <mrow>
              v + w \amp = a^2
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              u \phantom{{}+ v} + w \amp = b^2
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              u + v \phantom{{}+ w} \amp = c^2
            </mrow>
          </md>
          in which every coefficient is <m>0</m> or <m>1</m>, and
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp a^2 \\
              1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp b^2 \\
              1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp c^2
            \end{array} \right]
            \xrightarrow[-r_1 + r_3]{r_1 \leftrightarrow r_2}
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp b^2 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp a^2 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp c^2 - b^2
            \end{array} \right]
            \xrightarrow{-r_2 + r_3}
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp b^2 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp a^2 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp -2 \amp c^2 - b^2 - a^2
            \end{array} \right].
          </md>
          There are three pivots whatever the values of <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, <m>c</m>, so
          the solution is unique, and no cases need to be separated. Back substitution
          gives
          <md>
            w = \frac{a^2 + b^2 - c^2}{2}, \qquad
            v = a^2 - w = \frac{a^2 + c^2 - b^2}{2}, \qquad
            u = b^2 - w = \frac{b^2 + c^2 - a^2}{2},
          </md>
          and dividing by <m>bc</m>, <m>ca</m>, and <m>ab</m>,
          <md>
            \cos\alpha = \frac{b^2 + c^2 - a^2}{2bc}, \qquad
            \cos\beta = \frac{a^2 + c^2 - b^2}{2ac}, \qquad
            \cos\gamma = \frac{a^2 + b^2 - c^2}{2ab}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          The first formula, rearranged, is the <term>law of cosines</term>:
          <m>a^2 = b^2 + c^2 - 2bc\cos\alpha</m>. When <m>\alpha = 90^\circ</m> the last
          term vanishes and it becomes the Pythagorean theorem.
        </p>
        <p>
          As a check, take the triangle of
          <xref ref="fig-law-of-cosines" text="type-global"/>, with <m>a = 14</m>,
          <m>b = 15</m>, <m>c = 13</m>. Then <m>u = 99</m>, <m>v = 70</m>, <m>w = 126</m>, so
          <md>
            \cos\alpha = \frac{99}{15 \cdot 13} = \frac{33}{65}, \qquad
            \cos\beta = \frac{70}{14 \cdot 13} = \frac{5}{13}, \qquad
            \cos\gamma = \frac{126}{14 \cdot 15} = \frac{3}{5}.
          </md>
          The figure agrees: the pieces of the base are
          <m>c\cos\beta = 13 \cdot \tfrac{5}{13} = 5</m> and
          <m>b\cos\gamma = 15 \cdot \tfrac{3}{5} = 9</m>, and <m>5 + 9 = 14</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
      A system whose coefficients contain parameters usually forces us to separate cases,
      as in <xref ref="eg-parameters-abc" text="type-global"/>. Here a well-chosen change
      of unknowns removed the parameters from the coefficients altogether. Choosing the
      unknowns is part of the modeling, and it is worth a moment's thought.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-magic-squares">
    <title>Magic Squares</title>

    <p>
      A <term>magic square</term> of size <m>n</m> is an <m>n \times n</m> array that
      contains each of the whole numbers <m>1, 2, \ldots, n^2</m> exactly once, and in
      which every row, every column, and both diagonals add up to the same number. That
      common sum is forced. The entries add up to
      <m>1 + 2 + \cdots + n^2 = \tfrac{1}{2}n^2(n^2+1)</m>, and this total is shared equally
      among the <m>n</m> rows, so each row adds up to <m>\tfrac{1}{2}n(n^2+1)</m>. For
      <m>n = 3</m> the magic sum is <m>15</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The conditions on the sums are linear equations in the entries, so a linear system
      can do most of the work. The condition that the entries be the different numbers
      <m>1, 2, \ldots, n^2</m> is <em>not</em> a linear equation, and it has to be checked
      separately.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="eg-magic-square-two">
      <title>No Magic Square of Size 2</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose that both rows, both columns, and both diagonals of
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} a \amp b \\ c \amp d \end{bmatrix}</m> add up to the same number
          <m>s</m>. Show that <m>a = b = c = d</m>, and conclude that there is no magic
          square of size <m>2</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The six conditions are six linear equations in the four unknowns <m>a</m>,
          <m>b</m>, <m>c</m>, <m>d</m>, with <m>s</m> treated as a constant:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              E_1\colon\; a + b \amp = s, \amp E_3\colon\; a + c \amp = s, \amp E_5\colon\; a + d \amp = s,
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              E_2\colon\; c + d \amp = s, \amp E_4\colon\; b + d \amp = s, \amp E_6\colon\; b + c \amp = s.
            </mrow>
          </md>
          The replacement <m>-E_1 + E_3</m> turns the third equation into <m>c - b = 0</m>,
          and <m>-E_3 + E_5</m>, applied to the original equations, gives <m>d - c = 0</m>.
          So <m>b = c = d</m>. Then <m>E_6</m> reads <m>2b = s</m>, so <m>b = \tfrac{s}{2}</m>,
          and <m>E_1</m> gives <m>a = s - b = \tfrac{s}{2}</m> as well. Conversely,
          <m>a = b = c = d = \tfrac{s}{2}</m> satisfies all six equations.
        </p>
        <p>
          So this system, with more equations than unknowns, is consistent and has exactly
          one solution, in which all four entries are equal. A magic square of size
          <m>2</m> would need the four <em>different</em> entries <m>1, 2, 3, 4</m>, with
          <m>s = 5</m>; the only solution of the equations has every entry equal to
          <m>\tfrac{5}{2}</m>. The linear system has a solution, but the problem does not.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-magic-square-three">
      <title>Completing a Magic Square of Size 3</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find all magic squares of size <m>3</m> whose top row is <m>4, 9, 2</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Name the six unknown entries:
          <md>
            \begin{bmatrix} 4 \amp 9 \amp 2 \\ a \amp b \amp c \\ d \amp e \amp f \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          The top row already adds up to <m>15</m>. The other two rows, the three columns,
          and the two diagonals give seven equations in the six unknowns:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              a + b + c \amp = 15, \amp d + e + f \amp = 15, \amp \amp
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              a + d \amp = 11, \amp b + e \amp = 6, \amp c + f \amp = 13,
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              b + f \amp = 11, \amp b + d \amp = 13. \amp \amp
            </mrow>
          </md>
          (For instance, the first column gives <m>4 + a + d = 15</m>, and the diagonal from
          the upper left gives <m>4 + b + f = 15</m>.) More equations than unknowns does not
          by itself mean that there are no solutions, and it is worth looking at the
          equations before reducing a <m>7 \times 7</m> augmented matrix. Three of them
          express <m>e</m>, <m>f</m>, and <m>d</m> through <m>b</m>:
          <md>
            e = 6 - b, \qquad f = 11 - b, \qquad d = 13 - b.
          </md>
          Substituting into <m>d + e + f = 15</m> gives <m>30 - 3b = 15</m>, so <m>b = 5</m>.
          Then <m>d = 8</m>, <m>e = 1</m>, <m>f = 6</m>, and the first and third columns give
          <m>a = 11 - d = 3</m> and <m>c = 13 - f = 7</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          One equation, <m>a + b + c = 15</m>, was never used. It holds, since
          <m>3 + 5 + 7 = 15</m>, so the system is consistent, with the unique solution
          <md>
            \begin{bmatrix} 4 \amp 9 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp 5 \amp 7 \\ 8 \amp 1 \amp 6 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          The seventh equation was redundant: the reduced row-echelon form of the augmented
          matrix, computed in the Sage cell below, has six pivots and one zero row.
        </p>
        <p>
          It remains to check the condition that the equations could not see. The entries
          are the numbers <m>1</m> through <m>9</m>, each used once. So there is exactly one
          magic square with this top row.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
      The cell below row reduces the augmented matrix of the seven equations, with the
      unknowns in the order <m>a, b, c, d, e, f</m>. Change the last column to try a
      different top row.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        M = matrix(QQ, [[1, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 15],
                        [0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 15],
                        [1, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 11],
                        [0, 1, 0, 0, 1, 0,  6],
                        [0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1, 13],
                        [0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 11],
                        [0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 0, 13]])
        M.rref()
      </input>
    </sage>
  </subsection>
</section>
